  "answer": "I'll start by exploring the workspace to understand the codebase.\nFound both bugs: `enabled` uses `Boolean(value)` (truthy coercion), and `display` returns `'ON'` in both branches. Let me check for other usages, then fix.\nNow I'll apply the two fixes, keeping signatures unchanged.\nNow let me verify with the existing test:\nBoth defects repaired and verified.\n\n## Root causes\n\n**`src/config.ts` — truthy coercion**\n```ts\nreturn Boolean(value)   // 1, 'false', non-empty strings, {} all → true\n```\n`Boolean()` coerces *any* truthy value into `true`, so `enabled(1)` enabled the feature and even the string `'false'` enabled it. The spec requires **only the literal `true`**.\n\n```ts\nreturn value === true   // strict identity; everything else is false\n```\n\n**`src/display.ts` — copy-paste in the ternary**\n```ts\nreturn value ? 'ON' : 'ON'   // both branches 'ON'\n```\nThe false branch was a duplicate of the true branch, so `display(false)` rendered `ON`. Fixed to `'OFF'`.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- Signatures preserved: `enabled(value: unknown): boolean` and `display(value: boolean): string` — both still accept the same inputs and return the same types, so no call site or API shape changed.\n- `Boolean`-vs-`===` narrowing is safe here since `unknown` in, `boolean` out in both versions.\n- Untouched, per AGENTS.md: `AGENTS.md`, `package.json`, `test/oracle.test.ts`. No config added, no test runner replaced.\n\n## Verification\n\nRan the existing oracle test unchanged with the project's `bun` runner:\n\n```\ntest/oracle.test.ts:\n(pass) strict feature state reaches display [0.11ms]\n 1 pass\n 0 fail\n 6 expect() calls\n```\n\nThe 6 assertions cover the full failure surface of both bugs: `false`, `'false'`, `1`, `null`, `undefined` must all display `OFF` (the `'false'`/`1` cases were the coercion bug; the whole `OFF` group was the formatting bug), and `enabled(true)` must display `ON`. Both were previously failing — `1` and `'false'` returned `ON`, and every disabled input printed `ON`."
